You are given an integer array nums of length n.

An integer k is called sortable if k divides n and you can sort nums in non-decreasing order by sequentially performing the following operations:

Partition nums into consecutive subarrays of length k.
Cyclically rotate each subarray independently any number of times to the left or to the right.
Return an integer denoting the sum of all possible sortable integers k.

 

Example 1:

Input: nums = [3,1,2]

Output: 3

Explanation:​​​​​​​

For n = 3, possible divisors are 1 and 3.
For k = 1: each subarray has one element. No rotation can sort the array.
For k = 3: the single subarray [3, 1, 2] can be rotated once to produce [1, 2, 3], which is sorted.
Only k = 3 is sortable. Hence, the answer is 3.
Example 2:

Input: nums = [7,6,5]

Output: 0

Explanation:

For n = 3, possible divisors are 1 and 3.
For k = 1: each subarray has one element. No rotation can sort the array.
For k = 3: the single subarray [7, 6, 5] cannot be rotated into non-decreasing order.
No k is sortable. Hence, the answer is 0.
Example 3:

Input: nums = [5,8]

Output: 3

Explanation:​​​​​​​

For n = 2, possible divisors are 1 and 2.
Since [5, 8] is already sorted, every divisor is sortable. Hence, the answer is 1 + 2 = 3.
 

Constraints:

1 <= n == nums.length <= 10^5
1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5


Code:

/*

Time Complexity: O(n * tau(n))

The outer loop checks all n possible values of k. For each valid divisor of n, the inner loops scan at most n array elements. 
Since tau(n) is the number of divisors of n, the total time complexity is O(n * tau(n)).

*/

class Solution {
    public int sortableIntegers(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int ans = 0;

        for (int k = 1; k <= n; k++) {
            if (n % k != 0) {
                continue;
            }

            boolean valid = true;
            int prevMax = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

            for (int start = 0; start < n; start += k) {
                int end = start + k;
                int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
                int descents = 0;

                for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
                    min = Math.min(min, nums[i]);
                    max = Math.max(max, nums[i]);

                    int next = (i + 1 == end) ? start : i + 1;
                    if (nums[i] > nums[next]) {
                        descents++;
                    }
                }

                // This block must be sortable by cyclic rotation.
                if (descents > 1) {
                    valid = false;
                    break;
                }

                // Blocks must remain sorted relative to each other.
                if (prevMax > min) {
                    valid = false;
                    break;
                }

                prevMax = max;
            }

            if (valid) {
                ans += k;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

